y, throughout much of his reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "philip-i-of-croÿ-chimay", "sourceName": "Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "burgundian-wars", "targetName": "Burgundian Wars", "context": "Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay actively participated in various campaigns and conflicts that comprised the wider Burgundian Wars (1474-1477) against Swiss and French forces."}, {"sourceSlug": "philip-i-of-croÿ-chimay", "sourceName": "Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "house-of-croy", "targetName": "House of Croÿ", "context": "Philip I's strategic alliances and service solidified the House of Croÿ's position, influencing its future prominence under the Habsburgs."}], "places": [{"name": "Chimay, Belgium", "role": "Countship"}, {"name": "Brussels, Belgium", "role": "Burgundian court"}, {"name": "Neuss, Germany", "role": "Siege participation"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "philip-i-of-croÿ-chimay"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q506704", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_I_of_Cro%C3%BF-Chimay"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay played a significant regional role by faithfully serving the Dukes of Burgundy during their ambitious expansion, directly contributing to the military and administrative strength of the Burgundian State. His actions and strategic family alliances laid the groundwork for his descendants to become pivotal figures in the subsequent Habsburg Low Countries, ensuring the Croÿ family's long-term dynastic influence.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-01T07:04:52Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30688702885", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay, count of Chimay, Lord of Quiévrain, was a noble from the House of Croÿ, in the service of the Dukes of Burgundy.", "newValue": "Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay, who died on September 18, 1482, was a quintessential late medieval nobleman from the powerful House of Croÿ, serving as Count of Chimay and Lord of Quiévrain. His significance lies in his unwavering service to the Valois-Burgundy dukes, Philip the Good and Charles the Bold, during a pivotal era when the Burgundian State sought to establish itself as a dominant power between France and the Holy Roman Empire. As a high-ranking aristocrat and military commander, he embodied the feudal loyalty and strategic importance of such figures in the political and military landscape of the Low Countries and northeastern France.

Philip I actively participated in many of the ambitious military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvers that characterized the reigns of his Burgundian overlords. Notably, he was involved in the protracted and costly Siege of Neuss (1474-1475), a major engagement for Charles the Bold against the Archbishop of Cologne that significantly drained Burgundian resources. His marriage to Walburga of Moers and Saarwerden was a strategic alliance, expanding the Croÿ family's territorial and political reach into the Rhenish region and reinforcing their status within the complex web of European nobility.

